# NEXTEST'S KIBBLE — the first of its regime, and the end of a breach.
#
# The kennel spawns nextest for a collar whose runner declares it, and until this
# declaration stood it spawned whatever the station's path held: on this station,
# a copy in the station user's own cargo home, which the tackroom rule does not
# admit and which no record answered for. What stands here is the pin that closes
# that — which crate, which version, and one seal — and heel is the one door that
# turns it into a binary.
#
# THE SOURCE KIND, AND THE REASON IS A FACT ABOUT NEXTEST RATHER THAN A
# PREFERENCE. Its release ships no single manifest naming every archive by rust
# target triple, and it names its mac archive `universal-apple-darwin`, which no
# rustc triple says — so it cannot be fetched prebuilt. It asks 1.89 or above, no
# cmake, and only the C compiler rustc's own linker already needs, so it can be
# built here (BKSBL-Kibble.adoc "What sorts them").

BKRK_KIBBLE="bki_nextest"
BKRK_KIND="bknre_source"

# The crate as the registry publishes it, which is also the binary target cargo
# strikes and the name the binary wears at its residence. All three restating one
# word is the ordinary case rather than a redundancy to optimize away: they are
# three different facts that happen to agree here, and a door reading any of them
# out of an absence could not tell a declaration from an omission.
BKRK_PROGRAM="cargo-nextest"
BKRK_TARGET="cargo-nextest"
BKRK_BYNAME="cargo-nextest"

# THE VERSION HAS ONE HOME AND THIS IS IT. A constant naming it once stood in the
# leash beside the program's name; every road that spawns nextest now reads it
# from here, so a bump is this line and nothing else.
BKRK_VERSION="0.9.128"

# The archive's sha256, proven equal to the registry index's own checksum for
# this version on the bench (260906). Openssl's stdin form answers in lowercase
# hex and this is spelled the way that answer is spelled.
BKRK_SEAL="d8a41c206ff63dc9f9d45514ed3309adf4704ddd74f57dacacd5f7e613af9a98"

# The static host a published crate archive stands under. The version is NEVER
# typed in here — the door joins the program and the version onto this base — so
# that a bump cannot leave a stale URL behind pointing at an archive whose seal
# would then correctly refuse.
BKRK_REGISTRY="https://static.crates.io/crates"

# The channel this crate is BUILT at, which is the kibble's own and not the
# repository's. Nextest asks 1.89 or above; the estate's own pin is stated here
# so that one toolchain answers for the kennel and the program it spawns alike.
BKRK_CHANNEL="1.90.0"

# WHAT IS ASKED BACK. The regime builds every kibble with default features OFF so
# that one seal pins the whole closure; nextest's own default set is
# `default-no-update` plus `self-update`, and a program that can update itself is
# a program whose version stops being the one declared above. So the update
# feature is dropped by taking neither default and asking for the other by name.
BKRK_FEATURES="default-no-update"

# eof
